                <airSeal>

We had identified that your house is pretty air tight already and it is actually suffering from under-ventilation. Even so, there are a few pesky air leakage locations (all located at ceiling height) that are letting warm/moist air into your attic where significant frost buildup is occurring. I have already provided to you by email Infrared Images of the leakage locations and pictures of the frost buildup. There was extra information in that email, so please consider it, in addition to what I outline below.



** Both bathroom fans are older and allow air leakage through them. Newer ESTAR units have better butterfly dampers in them to restrict heat loss when not in use.



** The ceiling fan in the master bedroom needs to be properly sealed



** The attic hatch has alot of leakage and be sealed using a "removable" caulking such as "Draft Attack". You will want to do this after you fix the 2 bathroom exhaust fans and address the ceiling fan (both best sealed from inside the attic).



Savings: There should be about a 10% reduction in overall air leakage that, because your house is energy tight already, will likely be offset by the additional ventilation you will need to provide, perhaps by operating a bathroom fan a bit more (while partially or fully opening the outside fresh air supply pipe to the furnace) or perhaps if you choose to install a Heat Recovery Ventilation unit (see that section of my report). 



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 10% reduction I have modeled.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x6@16" with R20 insulation. We talked about your longer term plans to consider replacing the exterior siding of your house (and this would be a great time to add more exterior wall insulation).



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



I did mention that our DHERP will be expiring on March 31, 2024, so there is really only this summer &amp; next summer for you to complete this project .......... if you want to qualify through this program. Based on the estimated cost savings below vs. the cost of this BIG project, you may choose not to proceed with this and focus on the other energy saving &amp; indoor air quality enhancing measures outlined in this report :)



For your house, I modeled the adding another R10 to 100% of the exterior walls of the house, however you can add more if you want as your house has lots of soffit overhang.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 327 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,290 is available for adding R10 insulation to 100% of the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</mainWalls>
                <foundation />
                <windows>

All of the windows in your house are triple pane and appear to be well installed. The main floor windows have previously been upgraded and are of good quality. The 4 x basement windows are still in reasonably good shape (and well sealed), however you were possibly interested in upgrading them. If you proceed, I recommend you install ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 4 basement windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 12 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 1,000 in rebates (4 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality, however please first read my Ventilation section in the Additional Comments portion of this report.



I recommend that you consider having an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. I recommend you install one that is a minimum 75% efficient and has an ECM circulation motor to keep operation costs low.



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will increase the ventilation of your house (which is required), with an estimated $ 106 increase per year in energy costs, increasing your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. As electricity is quite expensive in Yellowknife and you essentially have a very large "electric kettle", we discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, could potentially save $ 875 per year in energy costs,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a very old 83% efficient, oil furnace. I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 507 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you could still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (South), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 4,006 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,322, and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two older toilets (one is a 6 litre and the other a 4.8 litre) that can be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush. Go online and check out some "Youtube" flush capacity videos.



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $8,500 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $1,000 for upgrading 4 windows with eligible ESTAR units

     - $3,000 for an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $   500 for a minimum10% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 Bonus if you achieve a minimum 20% total energy reduction 



For more information, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the range hood, 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 6.2 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may be expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace vents up a chimney and is susceptible to "back drafting".



During my assessment of your home, I noticed a combo smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detector on the main floor, but did not see either one in the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Heat Distribution



You mentioned that the basement is cold and you would like more warmth down there, either by having the furnace distribute the heat better or by installing a pellet or wood stove for the basement. Here are my thoughts, in conjunction with the new furnace upgrade recommendation:



        - The thermostat for the furnace is upstairs, so the furnace will always turn off based on that temperature.

              - There are some smart thermostats that can have 2 or more zones "communicate" with each other

        - There are 3 x return air supplies to the furnace (2 on main floor &amp; one in the basement)

              - Try partially closing the ones on the main floor so that more cold air is drawn from the basement 

        - Try closing down some of the main floor heat supply ducts to force more heat downstairs



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Installing a Pellet or Wood Stove in the Basement



Previously in this report, I modeled an upgrade to a 96% AFUE propane furnace that was estimated to provide about $507 of annual savings. We discussed your interest in perhaps installing a pellet stove or wood stove in the basement for some additional "zone" heat. If you install one, please ensure it has a dedicated and sealed outside combustion air supply pipe ........ as your house is quite air tight !!



Savings:

1. Install an 80% efficient Pellet Stove used 100% for heating the basement (furnace fan can be used to circulate throughout house)

     - Based on Bagged Pellets @ $5.99 each, the additional savings are estimated as $508/year

2. Install an 80% efficient Wood Stove used 100% for heating the basement (furnace fan can be used to circulate throughout house)

     - Based on Self-Harvested wood @ $100 per cord, the additional savings are estimated as $915/year



Rebates:

Installing as eligible wood or pellet stove can qualify for 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood (does not vent to the outside)

        - 2 older bathroom fans (read information in the email I sent you on Monday, March 29)

        - The clothes dryer

        - There is a 5" outside fresh air supply pipe connected to the furnace, however the damper is fully closed



The computer analyses of your house indicates that it is slightly under-ventilated. This can be enhanced by:

        - Having the range hood fan vent to the outside of the house

        - Install timer switches on the bathroom fans (see below)

        - Gradually opening the 5" fresh air supply damper to allow more "makeup air" into your house

        - If you are still having indoor air quality or "condensation on windows" issues, then think about installing an HRV 



As both bathroom fans are older, and allow a fair bit of air leakage, they should be replaced with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.40 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $1.00 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



